                <h2>When to Use This SOP</h2>
<p>This SOP applies when:</p>
<ul>
<li>Your TikTok Shop is launching new products and needs initial exposure fast</li>
<li>Your team is new to TikTok e-commerce and lacks reusable creative assets</li>
<li>Your budget is limited and you need focused testing to find effective creatives</li>
</ul>
<p>If your products are past the cold start phase, check our other article on scaling strategies.</p>
<h2>Step 1: Define Goals and Volume</h2>
<p>Before shooting, answer two key questions:</p>
<p><strong>How many assets do you need?</strong><br />
For cold start, prepare 30-50 raw clips, eventually filtering to 15-20 ad-ready versions. With 2-3 new products launching weekly, each requiring at least 5 distinct assets, your pool needs constant replenishment.</p>
<p><strong>What should these assets achieve?</strong><br />
The core goal during cold start is "validating the conversion path," not chasing virality. Set specific benchmarks:</p>
<ul>
<li>Organic engagement rate (likes + comments + shares) &gt; 3%</li>
<li>Product click-through rate &gt; 1.5%</li>
<li>Add-to-cart rate &gt; 0.8%</li>
</ul>
<blockquote>
<p>⚠️ Warning: Don't evaluate cold start assets with mature-phase ROI standards. This stage's mission is "helping the algorithm understand your product."</p>
</blockquote>
<h2>Step 2: Plan Your Creative Mix</h2>
<p>Don't put all eggs in one basket. Here's the recommended ratio for cold start:</p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Asset Type</th>
<th>Percentage</th>
<th>Notes</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Product Showcase</td>
<td>40%</td>
<td>Focus on use cases and key selling points</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>UGC-Style</td>
<td>30%</td>
<td>Simulate authentic user first-person perspectives</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Review/Comparison</td>
<td>20%</td>
<td>Works well for functional products needing trust</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Promotional</td>
<td>10%</td>
<td>Limited discounts, gifts—don't overdo it</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p><strong>Practical tip</strong>: Prioritize product showcase videos because they're efficient to produce and highly reusable. One utility app team found 3 qualifying assets out of 10 product showcase clips during cold start, then adapted the winning script logic for UGC-style shoots.</p>
<h2>Step 3: Establish Production Cadence</h2>
<p>Follow this timeline to avoid delays or wasted resources:</p>
<p><strong>Days 1-3: Script Planning</strong><br />
- List core product benefits, identify TOP 3<br />
- Prepare 2-3 script directions per asset type<br />
- Confirm filming equipment, locations, and talent</p>
<p><strong>Days 4-7: Batch Shooting</strong><br />
- Aim for 8-10 raw clips per day<br />
- Create at least 3 different opening hooks per product<br />
- Standard ratio: 9:16 vertical, 15-30 seconds duration</p>
<p><strong>Days 8-10: Editing &amp; Tagging</strong><br />
- Cut each raw clip into 2-3 versions (varying length, caption placement)<br />
- Name files with tags: product_type_hookstyle_date<br />
- Example: <code>smartwatch_ugc_question_0315</code></p>
<p><strong>Days 11-14: Upload &amp; Test</strong><br />
- Use Auto Campaign (Target Cost) mode first<br />
- Allocate $10-20 daily budget per asset<br />
- Review data after 72 hours, tag as qualified/optimize/pause</p>
<h2>Step 4: Track Performance and Tier Assets</h2>
<p>After launching, use this grading system:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Grade A</strong>: CTR &gt; 2%, Add-to-Cart &gt; 1% → Move to scaling campaigns</li>
<li><strong>Grade B</strong>: CTR 1-2%, room for optimization → Monitor 48 more hours</li>
<li><strong>Grade C</strong>: CTR &lt; 1%, or CPA 200%+ above target → Pause immediately</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key Metrics to Track</strong>:</p>
<ul>
<li>3-second completion rate (tests hook effectiveness)</li>
<li>Engagement rate (organic reach lever)</li>
<li>Conversion funnel data at each stage</li>
</ul>
<blockquote>
<p>💡 Tip: Log asset performance daily using a simple spreadsheet. Track Grade A/B trends—downward movement for 3 consecutive days demotes Grade B to Grade C.</p>
</blockquote>
<h2>Step 5: Asset Library Management &amp; Iteration</h2>
<p>Don't discard cold start assets when the phase ends. Build an archive system:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Archive Qualified Assets</strong>: Keep original project files with performance data</li>
<li><strong>Analyze Failed Assets</strong>: Document why they were eliminated to avoid repeats</li>
<li><strong>Extract Script Templates</strong>: Formalize winning structures for team reuse</li>
</ol>
<p>One cross-border home goods team extracted a "pain point question + product demo + limited-time offer" three-act template from 5 high-performing clips. When applied to new products, average spend per asset increased by 40%.</p>
<h2>FAQ</h2>
<p><strong>Q: Do cold start assets need professional production?</strong><br />
A: Not necessarily. TikTok audiences value authenticity—internal teams can shoot with smartphones initially. Focus on quickly validating product-market fit first, then upgrade production when you find winning directions.</p>
<p><strong>Q: Poor test results—is it the product or the creative?</strong><br />
A: Change the creative before changing the product. Cold start data samples are too small to judge product viability. Test at least 3 different creative directions before drawing conclusions.</p>
<p><strong>Q: Multiple new products launching simultaneously—how to allocate budget?</strong><br />
A: Concentrate resources testing the top 1-2 performers. Keep other products at minimal spend until you validate a winner, then replicate the winning creative structure across your portfolio.</p>
